        "reasoning": "Substituting n=1 gives 1+3(1)\\le4^1, namely 4\\le4. Assume 1+3k\\le4^k for an arbitrary integer k\\ge1. Substituting n=k+1 gives the target 1+3(k+1)\\le4^{k+1}. Indeed, 1+3(k+1)\\le4^k+3\\le4\\cdot4^k=4^{k+1}. The mathematics is certain, but the supplied worked example concerns a different inequality and does not establish Math Academy's key for this question.",
