    "step-q183419": {
      "key": "step-q183419",
      "question": {
        "dom_id": "step-q183419",
        "name": "Question 7",
        "problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that $2{n}^{2}>(n+1)^{2}$ for $n\\ge 3,n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=3.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}>{{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(k+1)^{2} & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+4k+2 & {{field-10}}{{field-11}} \\\\  & >{{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $2{n}^{2}>(n+1)^{2}$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}.",
        "worked_solution": "A variant of the principle of induction states the following: Let ${n}_{0}$ be an integer. Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P({n}_{0}),P({n}_{0}+1),P({n}_{0}+2),…,$ where\n\n- $P({n}_{0})$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every integer $k\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every integer $n\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2{n}^{2}>(n+1)^{2}\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n\\ge 3,$ the base case is $P(3).$ So, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)^{2}=18,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+1)^{2}=16,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 3.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 3,$ we have\n\n$$\n2{k}^{2}>(k+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n2(k+1)^{2}>(k+2)^{2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(k+1)^{2} & =2{k}^{2}+4k+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+4k+2 & ={{(2{k}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\ge (k+1)^{2}}+4k+2 \\\\  & >(k+1)^{2}+4k+2 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+{{2k+1}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 2}+4k+2 \\\\  & >{k}^{2}+4k+4 \\\\  & =(k+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $2{n}^{2}>(n+1)^{2}$ for all $n\\ge 3.$\n\nPROOF\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,$$3$.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)^{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\ngreater than\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ $k\\ge 3$, we have\n\n$$\n2{k}^{2}>(k+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(k+1)^{2} & =2{k}^{2}+4k+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+4k+2 & >(k+1)^{2}+4k+2 \\\\  & >{k}^{2}+2+4k+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =(k+2)^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $2{n}^{2}>(n+1)^{2}$ is true for all\n$n\\ge 3$.",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
