      "question": {
        "dom_id": "step-q183419",
        "name": "Question 7",
        "problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that $2{n}^{2}>(n+1)^{2}$ for $n\\ge 3,n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=3.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}>{{field-8}}.\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
