      ],
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275509/observations/step-e13227-2e15f6d2edbb646a6a56.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14275509/observations/step-e13227-2e15f6d2edbb646a6a56.png",
      "worked_solution": "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k \\in \\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2^n +1 \\leq 3^n \\quad\\text{for} \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n = 1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is less than or equal to the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n = 1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{1}+1=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{1}=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n = 1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n = k \\geq 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = k \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n2^k +1 \\leq 3^k.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n2^{k+1} + 1\\leq 3^{k+1}. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than or equal to the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{k+1}+1 & ={2}^{k}\\cdot {2}^{1}+1 \\\\  & =2({2}^{k})+1 \\\\  & ={2}^{k}+{2}^{k}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{k}+{{{2}^{k}+1}_{⏟}}_{\\le {3}^{k}} & \\le {2}^{k}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & ={{{2}^{k}}_{⏟}}_{<{3}^{k}<2\\cdot {3}^{k}}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & <2\\cdot {3}^{k}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {3}^{k} \\\\  & ={3}^{k+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is less than or equal to the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $2^n +1 \\leq 3^n$ for all $n \\geq 1.$",
      "can_dont_know": false,
      "can_skip": false,
      "question": {
        "dom_id": "step-e13227",
        "name": "Example: Proving Other Inequalities",
        "problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that $2^n +1 \\leq 3^n$ for $n\\in\\mathbb N.$",
        "worked_solution": "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k \\in \\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2^n +1 \\leq 3^n \\quad\\text{for} \\quad n \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n = 1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is less than or equal to the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n = 1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{1}+1=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{1}=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n = 1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n = k \\geq 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = k \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n2^k +1 \\leq 3^k.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n2^{k+1} + 1\\leq 3^{k+1}. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than or equal to the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{k+1}+1 & ={2}^{k}\\cdot {2}^{1}+1 \\\\  & =2({2}^{k})+1 \\\\  & ={2}^{k}+{2}^{k}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{k}+{{{2}^{k}+1}_{⏟}}_{\\le {3}^{k}} & \\le {2}^{k}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & ={{{2}^{k}}_{⏟}}_{<{3}^{k}<2\\cdot {3}^{k}}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & <2\\cdot {3}^{k}+{3}^{k} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {3}^{k} \\\\  & ={3}^{k+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is less than or equal to the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $2^n +1 \\leq 3^n$ for all $n \\geq 1.$",
